<!-- v2 (2026-11-16): rewritten for the rebuilt tool. The opening paragraph
     is the prose moved word for word from the 1.x screen header. -->
<h3 class="help first">What this tool does</h3>
<p class="help">The Audio Repair tool fixes .mp3 audio files that have been corrupted - often during download. Corrupted MP3 files identified by the Intelli-Tagger tool are re-built from RAW data using FFMPEG to ensure "Good In, Good Out" integrity.</p>

<h3>Where the list comes from</h3>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Unpack &amp; Convert</strong> and the <strong>Intelli-Tagger</strong> check every MP3 and fix small faults themselves. A file they can't fix is added here, with the reason.</li>
    <li>Unpack &amp; Convert also adds a lossless file (FLAC, WAV and so on) when converting it to MP3 failed.</li>
    <li>Each entry shows the file, its album and why it was added. <strong>Remove</strong> takes a file off the list without touching it, for example if you've replaced it yourself.</li>
</ul>

<h3>How a file is repaired</h3>
<ol>
    <li>MetaForge works on a <strong>copy</strong>, never on the file itself.</li>
    <li>An MP3 first has its damaged frames removed <strong>without re-encoding</strong>, so the sound quality and bitrate stay the same. If that isn't enough, the copy is re-encoded at V0 and the original's tags are copied onto it.</li>
    <li>A lossless file is converted to MP3 (V0) the same way Unpack &amp; Convert does it: same file name pattern, same tags. Run the Intelli-Tagger on that album afterwards.</li>
    <li>The copy is <strong>checked</strong>: it must play cleanly from start to finish and be within one second of the original's length.</li>
    <li>Only a copy that passes replaces the file. A copy that fails is thrown away, and the file stays on the list exactly as it was.</li>
</ol>
<p>Re-encoding can't add back quality that was never there: a 128 kbps file stays a 128 kbps recording, even at V0.</p>

<h3>Your originals</h3>
<p>Before a file is replaced, the original is copied to <code>%APPDATA%\MetaForge\backups\repair</code>, in a folder named with the date and time of the run, then the album. Nothing there is deleted automatically: remove the folder when you're happy with the repairs.</p>

<h3>Stopping and leaving</h3>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Stop</strong> ends the run after the current file. Everything repaired so far is kept, and the rest stays on the list.</li>
    <li>If you switch tools during a run, MetaForge asks first.</li>
    <li>Each repair is also written to the album's <code>MetaForge.log</code>.</li>
</ul>
<!-- --- END OF FILE help.mfi --- -->
